atients with a test that would  only be detected by deletion duplication  testing are having full sequencing and  holding they're told they're negative  and so we have some patients who haven't  had their ovaries removed who've gone on  to develop ovarian cancer other side of  the coin the interpretation is quite  complicated so some patients who  actually carry a variant of uncertain  significance which turns out to be a  polymorphism have been told that they  carry a mutation and believe it or not  we have patients who've had a bilateral  prophylactic mastectomy just like  angelina jolie did to later learn that  in fact they didn't carry a real  mutation we have other patients who've  had either hysterectomies or affected  means or vote for the same reason later  learning that they didn't need surgery  we've also had patients who have  completely different syndromes so two  sisters with ovarian cancer who are BRCA  negative and are told that it was  lightning striking twice when actually  when you expanded the history to four  generations it was Lynch syndrome and it  was clear that it was Lynch syndrome and  other family members died because the  wrong diagnosis was made so that was in  the world of brca1 and 2 testing this is  the world in which we now live now that  the market has opened up every other  laboratory is offering panel testing  which has huge benefits and a lot of  potential and one thing I'd like to  point out to you is that on this list is  Paul B to a gene that has recently  received a lot of press is being a new  gene brca2 point out that it's not a new  game we've known about it for years and  it's on many of these panels but I want  you to think for a moment if people were  confusing brca1 and 2 testing and they  can't order or interpret that accurately  what do you think is going to happen now  that we're offering these huge panels of  gene tests and that most of these games  we have much less information about then  we had about brca1 and 2 they're going  to be variants events  significance in these jeans and even for  people who really carry a mutation the  clinical recommendations about how to  follow those patients and are they  candidates for surveillance if so how  often what type how about chemo  prevention how about prophylactic  surgery it's getting much more  complicated so I think we can expect  that error rate unfortunately to go up  we know that some insurers cigna being  the first on a national level are now  actually requiring genetic counseling by  a certified provider before they will  consider the cost of some genetic tests  and this may be the wave of the future  that we're looking at a system that's  saying WOW clinicians unfortunately are  being encouraged by some of these  genetic testing companies to hang up a  shingle and be their own genetic  counselors the error rate is so high the  amount of money wasted of our healthcare  dollars is so high and then the results  are misinterpreted this doesn't make  sense we need to make sure that we're  ordering the right test and interpreting  it correctly not only for the patient in  front of you but for all of their family  members think of the long-term  ramifications for dozens sometimes  hundreds of family members and so the  last year has been really impactful for  the field of cancer genetic counseling  now that with the Angelina affect many  people know about genetic testing and  it's really brought it from an academic  clinical setting to the level of the  coffee shop that you can talk to people  in a coffee shop and say if you heard of  genetic testing and they have and so  what we're going to see moving forward  people being less afraid of genetic  testing more interested in genetic  testing which brings us to the next  issue which is direct-to-consumer  testing and I'm going to be using  23andme as an example today but there  are other companies that either exist  now or are in start-up mode who are  offering direct to consumer  testing and of course what this means is  that you can order a test a kit online  it's sent to the consumer in the mail  they can usually spit in a tube send it  back and have genetic testing performed  and so 23andme has had a lot of press  over the last few years some good some  bad for their vtc plans and last  Thanksgiving the FDA ruined your plans  to buy 23 in these tests as a Christmas  present by shutting them down in terms  of offering results for the genetic  testing they could still offer ancestry  in raw genetic data and so for ninety  nine dollars you can still get your  ancestry data in your rah genetic data  but no interpretation and so 23andme has  been grounded but i'd like to say  they're temporarily grounded and they  and other direct-to-consumer testing  companies have not gone away they're  just halted temporarily and what we know  is that some of these companies are  going to get it right they're going to  offer good testing at low prices with  accurate reports that consumers can  understand and it's going to be an  option for the future and there will be  pros and cons but one of them will get  it right and so with that I'd like to  invite you to type in your questions  about genetic counseling post scotus  gene patent decision and this incredible  last year of fast forward development in  the field  okay so we now have a question and this  comes from nicole thompson at howard  university cancer center do you think we  will eventually do away with single gene  or single syndrome testing and progress  toward panels for all high-risk  hereditary cancer patients and Nicole I  think there are pros and cons and I'm  sure you've seen them as well but i  think that the one-stop shopping we're  seeing with some testing companies  saying that they're going to go away  from single gene testing and only offer  panels it's probably not the answer of  course in a family with a known mutation  let's say a family with a reputation I  don't think it's really necessary in  most cases to offer a full pound it's  going to be more expensive we're going  to have variants of uncertain  significance that Papa I also think from  our experience at Yale over the last  year we were looking at panel testing  compared to single gene testing for  people who were presenting with what  looked like hereditary breast and  ovarian cancer syndrome and what we  found is that we had a lot of variants  of uncertain significance but very few  true mutations and jeans other than  brca1 or 2 when there wasn't a family  history that screamed leaf rimini or  that screamed you know hereditary  gastric cancer and breast cancer so I  think the jury is an out I don't think  that we should flip the switch and say  we're only going to do panel testing I  think we need to look at some data and  examine the costs as well as the pros  and the cons because if we're only  uncovering more variants of uncertain  significance we may be doing more harm  than good  you  okay well thank you very much for your  attention
 

